al lines = n = 5. We have used 3 non-sunny lines, leaving 2 lines to be sunny. So we have exactly 2 sunny lines left. Now we need to check if all points are covered.\n\nLet's list covered points:\n\n- Vertical x=1 covers (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5).\n- Diagonal x+y=6 covers (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1).\n- Diagonal x+y=5 covers (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1).\n\nNow combine:\n\nCovered points:\n\nColumn 1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5) all covered by vertical line.\n\nColumn 2: (2,1) not covered yet; (2,2) not covered; (2,3) covered by diagonal x+y=5; (2,4) covered by diagonal x+y=6.\n\nColumn 3: (3,1) not covered; (3,2) covered by diagonal x+y=5; (3,3) covered by diagonal x+y=6.\n\nColumn 4: (4,1) covered by diagonal x+y=5; (4,2) covered by diagonal x+y=6.\n\nColumn 5: (5,1) covered by diagonal x+y=6.\n\nThus uncovered points: (2,1), (2,2), (3,1). That's three points left. We have 2 sunny lines left to cover them. As before, can we cover these three points with 2 sunny lines? Let's see.\n\nPoints (2,1) and (3,1) are horizontal line y=1 (non-sunny). So cannot be covered together by a sunny line. Points (2,2) and (3,1) slope = (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, non-sunny. Points (2,1) and (2,2) vertical line x=2, non-sunny. So any pair is non-sunny. So we need 3 lines. So this construction fails.\n\nThus for n=5, k=2 seems impossible with this approach.\n\nBut maybe there is a different construction for n=5. Let's try to find any configuration with exactly 2 sunny lines for n=5.\n\nWe need 5 lines total, 2 sunny, 3 non-sunny. Non-sunny lines can be vertical/horizontal/diagonal. Let's try to use vertical lines for columns 1,2,3 (3 vertical lines). That's 3 non-sunny lines, using up all non-sunny lines. Then we have 2 sunny lines left to cover columns 4 and 5. Column 4 has points (4,1),(4,2). Column 5 has point (5,1). As before, we cannot cover these three points with 2 sunny lines because any line through (5,1) and (4,1) is horizontal (non-sunny), and through (5,1) and (4,2) is slope -1 (